      "sourceMarkdown": "*(Source: pp. 11–12)*\n\n### Background (Hinnele):\nIn the celestial realm of Kailāsa, Goddess Pārvatī respectfully asked Lord Śiva to reveal a sacred vrata that bestows upon women all enduring marital auspiciousness (*saubhāgya*), virtuous progeny (*santāna*), and the complete eradication of poverty and adversity (*dāridrya-parihāra*). In response, Lord Śiva extolled the unmatched glory and efficacy of the **Jyeṣṭhā Lakṣmī Vrata**. On earth, eminent sages assembled on the sacred banks of the Gaṅgā propagated its liturgical procedure for the upliftment of humanity.\n\n### The Sacred Narrative (Kathe):\nIn the city of Jayavardhana lived a brāhmaṇa of good conduct named Subhadra and his devoted wife Suvratā. Distressed by severe poverty, they departed for the forest.\n\nThere, on a riverbank, Suvratā saw women performing the Jyeṣṭhā Lakṣmī Vrata. Following the guidance of sage Vasiṣṭha, she observed the vrata faithfully on Bhādrapada Śukla Aṣṭamī and worshiped the sixteen-knotted sacred cord (*dora*).\n\n### The Divine Fruit & Blessing (Phala):\nBy the grace of Śrī Mahālakṣmī, their poverty ended. They obtained a fine home filled with wealth and grain, and noble children.\n\n**Phalaśruti:** The booklet says that those who faithfully perform this vrata and hear its story are freed from sins and attain auspicious good fortune.\n"
